//
// `Type::source_name` exists because `Type::name` is the SCHEMA KEY and not a renderer: it
// spells a keyed collection `hash<It,["k"]>` where the author wrote `hash<It[k]>`, and the two
// are kept apart on purpose — re-spelling a type in `name` re-IDENTIFIES it, which is what
// generated `init()` replays.
//
// `source_name` carried the keyed arms and no WRAPPER arms, so `Optional`, `RefVar` and
// `Rewritten` fell to `_ => self.name(data)` and `name` re-spelled the payload they wrap.  The
// debug spelling leaked through the one character the author added: `hash<It[k]>?` reached the
// reader as `hash<It,["k"]>?`, and `&hash<Row[id]>` as `&hash<Row,["id"]>` (loft#1434,
// loft#1445).  The arms now recurse into `source_name`.
//
// Guarded here rather than left to review because nothing else fails when a message is merely
// unreadable — the compiler is right, the program is wrong, and only the author pays, which is
// exactly how this drifted in the first place.
//
// ⚠ This cell is the `Optional` wrapper and is chosen for a mechanical reason: it is the one
// nullable-keyed shape that emits exactly ONE diagnostic.  `for e in h` over the same type
// draws the refusal AND two cascade errors, and a refusal file must pin exactly one — the
// constraint `1067b-lambda-no-expected-type-refused.loft` records.  The `RefVar` arm is scored
// by `parse_errors::a_reference_to_a_keyed_collection_is_named_as_written`.
//
// @falsified-at: none — this is a message-SPELLING guard, and the harness scores it by exact
//   match: the pre-fix build emits `hash<It,["k"]>?` where the `@EXPECT_WARNING` below says
//   `hash<It[k]>?`, so the cell fails on the control by construction.  A falsify run would add
//   nothing a reader cannot see from the two strings.

struct It { k: integer, v: integer }

fn take(h: hash<It[k]>) -> integer { len(h) }

// @EXPECT_WARNING: a nullable `hash<It[k]>?` is stored into parameter 1 of `take` of the non-null type `hash<It[k]>` — it becomes null there; discharge with `?` (the type's default), `?? <default>`, or `match` if that is not intended
fn a_keyed_type_is_named_as_the_source_writes_it() {
  h: hash<It[k]>? = [It { k: 2, v: 20 }];
  assert_eq("{take(h)}", "1", "the store proceeds — this cell is about the MESSAGE");
}
